"""
Behavioural Competencies Question Bank: exactly 34 Easy, 33 Medium, 33 Hard = 100 questions.
Focus: Competency Models (Iceberg Model), STAR Method, Emotional Intelligence, Team Dynamics (Belbin Roles), Accountability, Ethics, and Adaptability.
"""

beh_easy = [
    ("In the Spencer & Spencer 'Competency Iceberg Model', which components lie above the waterline (easily observed and developed)?", "Skills and Knowledge", ["Values and Social Role", "Self-Image and Traits", "Underlying Motives and Drives"], "The Iceberg model posits that technical knowledge and skills are visible on the surface, while deeper traits and motives lie hidden underwater."),
    ("In the Competency Iceberg Model, which components lie deep beneath the waterline (hardest to assess and change)?", "Core Motives, Personal Traits, and Underlying Values", ["Computer typing speed and software knowledge", "Academic certificates and formal degrees", "Physical height and office dress style"], "Deeper behavioral drivers like core motives and personality traits require structured behavioral assessment to evaluate."),
    ("What does the 'STAR' method stand for in competency-based behavioral interviewing?", "Situation, Task, Action, and Result", ["Strategy, Target, Assessment, and Review", "Seniority, Training, Attitude, and Reliability", "Skill, Talent, Aptitude, and Readiness"], "The STAR framework guides candidates to provide concrete, structured evidence of past actions rather than vague hypothetical claims."),
    ("In the STAR interview response framework, what does the 'Action' component focus on?", "The specific steps, behaviors, and initiatives YOU personally took to resolve the problem", ["The actions taken by external third-party contractors", "The general philosophy of the corporate industry", "The weather conditions on the day the incident happened"], "Interviewers evaluate the candidate's personal agency, competence, and leadership through the concrete actions described."),
    ("What is 'Accountability' as a core workplace behavioral competency?", "Taking personal responsibility for one's actions, decisions, deliverables, and resulting outcomes without making excuses", ["Blaming colleagues whenever a project milestone is missed", "Escalating all routine decisions to senior directors", "Refusing to sign any document that carries legal risk"], "Accountable individuals own their results—both successes and failures—demonstrating reliability and integrity."),
    ("What does 'Integrity' mean as an organizational behavioral competency?", "Adhering consistently to moral, ethical, and professional principles even when unobserved or under pressure", ["Obeying whatever orders produce the highest quarterly profit", "Doing the right thing only when the supervisor is watching", "Telling people whatever they want to hear to avoid conflict"], "Integrity creates foundational trust: words match actions, ethical guidelines are respected, and honesty is uncompromising."),
    ("What is 'Adaptability' in contemporary workplace competencies?", "The ability to adjust one's thinking, behaviors, and work methods flexibly in response to changing conditions and new information", ["Resisting any change to standard historical office routines", "Changing corporate passwords every single morning", "Changing one's core ethical values to please clients"], "Adaptable professionals embrace uncertainty, learn new tools quickly, and thrive amid structural disruptions."),
    ("What is 'Empathy' in workplace interpersonal competencies?", "The capacity to understand, resonate with, and consider others' emotional perspectives and needs during interactions", ["Crying publicly whenever a colleague receives a reprimand", "Agreeing to every unreasonable demand made by customers", "A psychological weakness that prevents decisive management"], "Empathy enhances team cohesion, de-escalates interpersonal friction, and enables compassionate customer service."),
    ("What is 'Resilience' as a behavioral competency?", "The capacity to recover quickly from setbacks, adapt to adversity, and maintain focus and composure under sustained pressure", ["Ignoring all physical fatigue and working until total collapse", "Refusing to acknowledge that a mistake has occurred", "Quitting immediately when the first obstacle appears"], "Resilient individuals reframe failures as learning opportunities, maintaining psychological stamina and optimism."),
    ("What does 'Customer Centricity' (or Citizen Centricity) require from a professional?", "Placing the genuine needs, satisfaction, and long-term value of the user at the core of all operational decisions", ["Offering free unlimited luxury gifts to commercial clients", "Doing whatever the customer says even if it violates the law", "Treating customers with hostility to protect company profits"], "Customer-centric behavior listens deeply, delivers empathetic service, and proactively removes user pain points."),
    ("What is 'Active Listening' as a behavioral indicator in communication competencies?", "Giving undivided attention, confirming understanding through paraphrasing, and suspending premature judgment", ["Writing down notes while planning an aggressive counter-argument", "Interrupting the speaker frequently to demonstrate high intelligence", "Nodding continuously while daydreaming about evening dinner"], "Active listeners seek first to understand, validating the speaker and fostering a collaborative dialogue."),
    ("What is 'Conflict Resolution' competency primarily focused on?", "Managing and resolving interpersonal or professional disagreements constructively to reach mutually beneficial outcomes", ["Suppressing all dissenting opinions through threat of termination", "Winning every argument by shouting louder than the other party", "Ignoring disagreements permanently until one employee resigns"], "Effective conflict resolvers identify underlying needs, maintain emotional composure, and facilitate collaborative win-win solutions."),
    ("What is 'Continuous Learning' (Growth Orientation) as a personal competency?", "Actively seeking opportunities to acquire new skills, knowledge, and perspectives to adapt to future challenges", ["Enrolling in formal university degree courses forever without working", "Memorizing the company telephone directory by heart", "Believing that one already knows everything needed for one's career"], "Continuous learners proactively identify capability gaps, embrace feedback, and experiment with novel approaches."),
    ("What is 'Collaboration' as a team behavioral competency?", "Working cooperatively and constructively with diverse individuals across boundaries toward shared organizational goals", ["Doing all project tasks completely alone to ensure zero mistakes", "Competing against teammates to make them look incompetent", "Delegating all tedious manual work to junior interns"], "Collaboration values complementary strengths, shares credit generously, and dismantles departmental silos."),
    ("What does 'Self-Regulation' (Emotional Composure) mean during high-stress workplace crises?", "Maintaining emotional control, avoiding impulsive outbursts, and making rational, measured decisions under acute pressure", ["Bottling up extreme rage until a violent public explosion occurs", "Demonstrating zero emotional empathy toward human distress", "Laughing inappropriately during serious safety accidents"], "Composed professionals manage stress triggers, project calm confidence, and steady anxious teams during turbulent crises."),
    ("What is 'Attention to Detail' as a behavioral competency?", "Thoroughness, precision, and diligence in accomplishing tasks, ensuring accuracy across both micro and macro levels", ["Obsessively proofreading an email 50 times and missing the deadline", "Focusing on minor spelling errors while ignoring major strategy flaws", "Demanding that all office furniture be aligned with laser rulers"], "Detail-oriented individuals catch critical discrepancies, uphold rigorous quality standards, and deliver error-free work."),
    ("What does 'Initiative' (Proactiveness) signify in employee behavior?", "Recognizing what needs to be done and acting independently before being instructed or forced by events", ["Waiting passively for direct orders from a supervisor before acting", "Violating safety protocols to finish a task five minutes early", "Taking credit for a project completed by someone else"], "Proactive employees spot emerging problems, initiate preventative actions, and propose innovative enhancements voluntarily."),
    ("What is 'Time Management' as a personal productivity competency?", "Planning and exercising conscious control over time spent on specific activities to increase efficiency and effectiveness", ["Working 18 hours a day without taking breaks or sleeping", "Doing whatever task was received most recently in your email inbox", "Arriving at meetings exactly when the meeting is concluding"], "Effective time managers prioritize strategic tasks, eliminate time-wasters, and maintain healthy professional boundaries."),
    ("What does 'Cultural Sensitivity' (Cross-Cultural Competence) mean in global workplaces?", "Understanding, respecting, and adapting behavior appropriately to interact effectively with people from diverse cultural backgrounds", ["Assuming your own culture's customs are the universal standard for all", "Forcing foreign colleagues to speak your native regional language", "Avoiding any interaction with colleagues from foreign branches"], "Cultural competence avoids ethnocentrism, appreciates diverse perspectives, and navigates cross-cultural nuances diplomatically."),
    ("What is 'Assertiveness' in interpersonal behavioral competencies?", "Communicating thoughts, needs, and boundaries clearly, directly, and respectfully without becoming aggressive or passive", ["Aggressively insulting colleagues who disagree with your opinion", "Remaining silent and accepting unfair workloads without complaint", "Using sarcastic jokes to mock team members behind their backs"], "Assertive communicators stand up for their rights while honoring the dignity and perspectives of others."),
    ("What is 'Decision-Making' competency?", "The ability to analyze information, evaluate alternatives, weigh risks, and make timely, sound, and actionable choices", ["Postponing choices indefinitely until someone else makes the decision", "Flipping a coin to determine corporate strategic acquisitions", "Choosing the option that brings personal popularity regardless of logic"], "Effective decision-makers synthesize ambiguous data, balance speed with rigor, and take responsibility for consequences."),
    ("What does 'Stakeholder Management' involve as a professional competency?", "Identifying, engaging, and maintaining productive relationships with individuals or groups affected by an initiative", ["Bribing government regulatory officials to ignore safety laws", "Only communicating with the single most senior executive in the company", "Avoiding all contact with customers to focus purely on coding"], "Stakeholder managers map diverse interests, align expectations, communicate proactively, and build enduring trust."),
    ("What is 'Influencing Skills' (Persuasion) in leadership behavior?", "The ability to guide, persuade, and convince others to support an idea, project, or vision without relying on coercive power", ["Threatening to fire anyone who refuses to support your proposal", "Offering secret personal cash kickbacks to decision-makers", "Giving lengthy lectures until listeners concede out of total boredom"], "Skilled influencers use empathy, logical evidence, compelling storytelling, and shared values to build authentic commitment."),
    ("What is 'Coaching and Mentoring' as a leadership developmental competency?", "Guiding, supporting, and empowering individuals to build capabilities, realize their potential, and achieve career goals", ["Doing the employee's work for them whenever they make a mistake", "Giving harsh public reprimands to demoralize poor performers", "Forcing employees to copy your exact personal habits and beliefs"], "Coaches ask empowering questions, offer developmental feedback, and create growth opportunities for team members."),
    ("What is 'Strategic Thinking' competency?", "The ability to analyze broader environmental trends, visualize long-term possibilities, and translate vision into actionable strategies", ["Focusing exclusively on today's routine administrative tasks", "Memorizing yesterday's closing stock market prices", "Refusing to change standard operational procedures from 20 years ago"], "Strategic thinkers anticipate market disruptions, connect disparate dots, and align daily actions with future competitive goals."),
    ("What is 'Problem Solving' as a core behavioral competency?", "The capacity to identify root causes of complex issues, generate creative alternatives, and implement effective remedies", ["Ignoring operational defects until a customer files a lawsuit", "Blaming the equipment manufacturer whenever a machine stops running", "Applying superficial quick-fix band-aids that fail after three days"], "Problem solvers apply structured methodologies (e.g., 5 Whys, Ishikawa) to eradicate problems permanently."),
    ("What is 'Constructive Feedback' in workplace performance interactions?", "Specific, actionable, and behavioral information provided with supportive intent to help a person improve performance", ["Publicly mocking someone's personality traits in front of the team", "Vague praise like 'Good job' without explaining what was done well", "A list of personal insults delivered during an angry outburst"], "Constructive feedback focuses on observable behaviors and objective impacts, offering clear pathways for growth."),
    ("What does 'Dependability' (Reliability) mean in professional behavior?", "Consistently meeting commitments, adhering to deadlines, and producing high-quality work without requiring constant supervision", ["Arriving at work on time only on days when bonuses are distributed", "Promising to complete ten tasks and delivering only one partially", "Doing tasks only when a manager is physically standing over your desk"], "Dependable professionals are the reliable anchors of organizations, trusted by peers and leaders to follow through."),
    ("What is 'Open-Mindedness' as a cognitive behavioral competency?", "Receptivity to new ideas, unconventional perspectives, and constructive criticism without immediate defensive rejection", ["Changing your opinions every 10 minutes depending on who spoke last", "Refusing to adhere to established scientific facts and laws", "Believing every rumor published on unverified social media forums"], "Open-minded professionals welcome diverse cognitive viewpoints, stress-testing assumptions to arrive at robust truths."),
    ("What is 'Workplace Ethics' as a behavioral standard?", "The application of moral standards, honesty, fairness, and social responsibility in organizational operations and decisions", ["Maximizing company quarterly profits through any legal loophole available", "Following company policies only when audited by external regulators", "Keeping fraudulent balance sheet accounting errors strictly secret"], "Workplace ethics transcends minimum legal compliance, embodying moral duty and institutional respect."),
    ("What is 'Stress Management' as a self-leadership competency?", "Deploying healthy physical, cognitive, and emotional coping mechanisms to mitigate the negative impacts of chronic workplace tension", ["Smoking 30 cigarettes a day and drinking excessive alcohol after work", "Denying that stress exists while suffering from high blood pressure", "Shouting at junior employees to release internal emotional anger"], "Effective stress managers practice mindfulness, boundary-setting, physical wellness, and cognitive reframing."),
    ("What does 'Negotiation' competency involve in commercial partnerships?", "Conferring with others to reach mutually agreeable compromises, settlements, and contracts that satisfy essential underlying interests", ["Demanding 100% of your initial claims and walking out if refused", "Surrendering all company rights immediately to avoid any conflict", "Misrepresenting financial figures to trick the other party into signing"], "Skilled negotiators identify shared interests, expand value options, and forge resilient long-term partnerships."),
    ("What is 'Change Management' behavioral capability?", "The ability to champion, facilitate, and guide individuals through the emotional and operational transitions of organizational change", ["Announcing structural reorganizations abruptly via email without warning", "Ignoring employee anxiety and forcing compliance through fear", "Refusing to adopt modern computer software to protect old paper files"], "Change champions communicate compelling rationales, validate emotional resistance, and anchor new habits into culture."),
    ("What is 'Service Orientation' in public administration competencies?", "A genuine commitment to serving citizens efficiently, transparently, courteously, and equitably", ["Viewing citizens as annoying interruptions to routine paperwork", "Demanding unofficial speed-money payments to process citizen files", "Keeping administrative service windows closed during public hours"], "Public service orientation treats citizens with dignity, upholds public trust, and strives for responsive, accessible governance.")
]

beh_med = [
    ("In Dr. Meredith Belbin's Team Roles framework, what are the three overarching categories of roles?", "Action-Oriented Roles, People-Oriented Roles, and Thought-Oriented Roles", ["Executive Roles, Secretarial Roles, and Laborer Roles", "Technical Roles, Financial Roles, and Marketing Roles", "Morning Roles, Afternoon Roles, and Night Roles"], "Belbin identified 9 team roles distributed across action (driving, doing), people (collaborating), and thought (analyzing, creating)."),
    ("In Belbin's framework, what is the 'Plant' role primarily known for?", "Highly creative, unorthodox, imaginative, and a solver of difficult, complex problems (Thought-oriented)", ["Disciplined, reliable, and organizing practical implementation", "Outgoing, enthusiastic, and exploring external commercial networks", "Painstaking, conscientious, and searching out errors and omissions"], "Plants provide innovative intellectual sparks, though they may overlook practical operational details or communicate abstractly."),
    ("In Belbin's framework, what is the 'Monitor Evaluator' role characterized by?", "Sober, strategic, discerning, and objectively judging all options with detached critical analysis (Thought-oriented)", ["Inspiring the team emotionally with charismatic speeches", "Rushing impulsively to implement ideas without testing feasibility", "Causing heated interpersonal arguments during team meetings"], "Monitor Evaluators prevent teams from pursuing flawed projects, though they can sometimes appear skeptical or uninspiring."),
    ("In Belbin's framework, what does the 'Completer Finisher' role do?", "Painstaking, conscientious, anxious about errors, searches out omissions, and polishes work to perfection (Action-oriented)", ["Proposing radical, untested conceptual theories at kickoff", "Negotiating supply chain contracts with external vendors", "Introducing new team members during morning orientation sessions"], "Completer Finishers ensure rigorous quality control and on-time delivery, though they may struggle to delegate or worry excessively."),
    ("In Belbin's framework, what characterizes the 'Resource Investigator' role?", "Extroverted, enthusiastic, communicative, and explores external opportunities, ideas, and contacts (People-oriented)", ["Sitting in a closed room analyzing complex mathematical spreadsheets", "Refusing to speak to anyone outside the immediate departmental team", "Polishing final written documents to eliminate minor typos"], "Resource Investigators are natural networkers who bring outside ideas into the team, though enthusiasm can fade quickly."),
    ("In Belbin's framework, what is the 'Shaper' role characterized by?", "Challenging, dynamic, thrives on pressure, and possesses drive and courage to overcome obstacles (Action-oriented)", ["Gentle, quiet, and avoiding any form of conflict or debate", "Focusing on administrative filing and routine compliance paperwork", "Analyzing philosophical moral ethics of corporate business"], "Shapers inject relentless drive and urgency, but risk bruising feelings or generating friction during stressful pushes."),
    ("In Belbin's framework, what characterizes the 'Coordinator' role?", "Mature, confident, clarifies goals, delegates effectively, and facilitates decision-making (People-oriented)", ["Writing software code in total isolation without speaking", "Inspecting machinery parts for microscopic structural defects", "Generating radical artistic designs for company advertising"], "Coordinators clarify objectives and bring out the best in diverse teammates without dominating discussions autocratically."),
    ("In Belbin's framework, what does the 'Teamworker' role contribute?", "Cooperative, perceptive, diplomatic, listens, averts friction, and calms interpersonal tensions (People-oriented)", ["Challenging every proposal aggressively to test leader resolve", "Refusing to listen to other people's emotional complaints", "Focusing exclusively on mechanical engineering calculations"], "Teamworkers serve as the emotional glue of the group, fostering harmony and psychological safety, though they may avoid tough choices."),
    ("In Belbin's framework, what does the 'Implementer' role do?", "Practical, reliable, disciplined, and turns abstract concepts and plans into workable, concrete actions (Action-oriented)", ["Daydreaming about future space travel during project meetings", "Constantly changing project plans every single afternoon", "Seeking external venture capital investment for new startups"], "Implementers build operational workflows and systematic routines, though they can be slow to adapt to sudden changes."),
    ("In Belbin's framework, what characterizes the 'Specialist' role?", "Single-minded, self-starting, dedicated, and provides rare specialized knowledge and technical expertise (Thought-oriented)", ["Managing broad cross-functional HR and payroll operations", "Focusing on motivational team-building outdoor retreat games", "Negotiating commercial bank loans with financial directors"], "Specialists contribute deep domain mastery on technical frontiers, but may dwell on technicalities rather than the big picture."),
    ("What does 'VUCA' stand for in contemporary strategic and leadership environments?", "Volatility, Uncertainty, Complexity, and Ambiguity", ["Velocity, Utility, Capital, and Allocation", "Verification, Universal, Compliance, and Auditing", "Vision, Unity, Courage, and Action"], "Coined by the US Army War College, VUCA describes turbulent environments requiring vision, understanding, clarity, and agility."),
    ("In a VUCA world, what strategic leadership quality counters 'Volatility'?", "'Vision' (a clear, compelling north star and core purpose that steadies the organization amid turbulence)", ["'Control' (micromanaging daily employee task checklists)", "'Secrecy' (keeping all financial records completely hidden)", "'Denial' (pretending that market fluctuations do not exist)"], "Bob Johansen's VUCA Prime framework matches Volatility with Vision: enduring long-term intent anchors teams during erratic shocks."),
    ("In the VUCA Prime model, what leadership capability counters 'Uncertainty'?", "'Understanding' (gathering deep intelligence, listening to diverse signals, and synthesizing contextual insight)", ["'Rigidity' (enforcing standard historical operating manuals)", "'Isolation' (refusing to talk to customers or suppliers)", "'Speed' (making blind split-second guesses without data)"], "Understanding pierces uncertainty by actively exploring weak signals, scenario planning, and challenging obsolete mental models."),
    ("In the VUCA Prime model, what leadership capability counters 'Complexity'?", "'Clarity' (simplifying communications, identifying essence, and focusing on the vital few high-impact levers)", ["'Confusion' (using convoluted academic terminology)", "'Bureaucracy' (adding seven additional administrative sign-off tiers)", "'Diversification' (launching 50 unrelated business ventures at once)"], "Clarity cuts through chaotic complexity, providing unambiguous priorities and transparent operating frameworks."),
    ("In the VUCA Prime model, what capability counters 'Ambiguity'?", "'Agility' (flexible, rapid experimentation, rapid prototyping, and iterative learning from failure)", ["'Hesitation' (waiting five years for absolute 100% certainty)", "'Dogmatism' (insisting on fixed, unalterable doctrine)", "'Punishment' (firing anyone whose experimental pilot fails)"], "Agility navigates ambiguity by probing, sensing, adapting rapidly, and failing fast and cheap to discover workable paths."),
    ("What are the five facets of 'Learning Agility' in executive talent development (Lombardo and Eichinger)?", "Mental Agility, People Agility, Change Agility, Results Agility, and Self-Awareness", ["Math Agility, Reading Agility, Writing Agility, Physical Agility, and Speed", "Coding Agility, Accounting Agility, Legal Agility, Tax Agility, and Audit", "Memory Agility, Speech Agility, Hearing Agility, Vision Agility, and IQ"], "Learning agility—the ability to learn rapidly from experience and apply that learning to novel conditions—is a top predictor of potential."),
    ("In learning agility, what is 'Change Agility'?", "Being curious, comfortable with experimentation, and enthusiastically leading transformational change initiatives", ["Changing one's personal wardrobe twice every business day", "Constantly switching between different computer hardware brands", "Altering project financial balance sheets before an audit"], "Change-agile leaders enjoy challenging the status quo, embrace progressive experiments, and adapt readily to turbulence."),
    ("In learning agility, what is 'Results Agility'?", "Delivering exceptional outcomes in first-time, ambiguous situations by inspiring teams and demonstrating resourcefulness", ["Completing familiar, repetitive factory tasks at high speed", "Ensuring that company quarterly profits match historical averages", "Submitting daily timesheets ahead of the Friday deadline"], "Results agility achieves breakthrough outcomes under tough conditions by marshalling resources creatively."),
    ("In learning agility, what is 'Mental Agility'?", "Thinking critically, comfortably handling complexity, and discovering unique trends and fresh connections across disparate data", ["Memorizing hundreds of complex dictionary definitions", "Solving Sudoku number puzzles during lunch breaks", "Repeating the same standard solution to every problem"], "Mentally agile professionals unravel complexity, reframe dilemmas, and identify novel solutions where others see chaos."),
    ("In learning agility, what is 'People Agility'?", "Understanding diverse individuals, connecting deeply across boundaries, and building high-trust collaborative partnerships", ["Managing large physical crowds at public sporting events", "Speaking on loudspeaker systems at railway stations", "Giving orders to hundreds of automated factory assembly robots"], "People-agile leaders relate constructively to varied personalities, handle tough feedback gracefully, and resolve friction."),
    ("What is 'Behavioral Event Interviewing' (BEI) (David McClelland)?", "An in-depth assessment technique that asks candidates to narrate specific detailed stories of how they handled real past situations", ["An interview where candidates perform physical stage acting roleplays", "A lie-detector polygraph test conducted on job applicants", "A written examination testing knowledge of company historical trivia"], "BEI uncovers unconscious competencies by probing actual thoughts, feelings, and concrete actions taken during critical past events."),
    ("What is the underlying premise of Behavioral Interviewing?", "'Past behavioral performance in similar situations is the best and most reliable predictor of future performance'", ["'A candidate who dresses in luxury suits will always be ethical'", "'A person with a master's degree knows how to handle every crisis'", "'Candidates who make bold promises will always deliver them'"], "Past behavior demonstrates established habits and emotional instincts, proving far more predictive than hypothetical intentions."),
    ("What is an 'In-Basket (In-Tray) Exercise' used in Assessment Centers?", "A simulation where candidates prioritize and resolve a collection of realistic emails, memos, and urgent operational dilemmas within a tight deadline", ["A physical test where candidates throw basketballs into wastebaskets", "A test evaluating an applicant's ability to carry heavy office boxes", "A shopping simulation purchasing groceries from retail catalogs"], "In-basket exercises assess prioritization, decision-making, delegation, and written communication under time constraints."),
    ("What is a 'Leaderless Group Discussion' (LGD) in assessment centers?", "A group simulation where candidates collaborate to solve an ambiguous problem without any assigned leader, revealing emergent leadership and teamwork", ["A meeting where the CEO leaves the room because of an emergency", "A group argument where participants shout at each other without rules", "A silent meditation session conducted in a dark room"], "LGDs reveal natural influencers, diplomatic facilitators, active listeners, and those whose aggressive dominance stifles collaboration."),
    ("What is 'Role Play' simulation in behavioral competency evaluation?", "An interactive simulation where the candidate interacts with a trained actor playing a challenging stakeholder (e.g., angry customer or underperforming peer)", ["An entertaining comedy performance staged at corporate parties", "A theatrical drama audition for commercial television movies", "A military exercise practicing physical combat self-defense"], "Role plays evaluate real-time emotional composure, empathy, assertiveness, and active listening in emotionally charged scenarios."),
    ("What is 'Emotional Labor' (Arlie Hochschild) in customer-facing roles?", "The psychological effort required to induce, suppress, or display specific organizationally mandated emotions (e.g., smiling at rude customers)", ["Physical manual labor performed in extreme summer heat", "The financial accounting of corporate employee salaries", "Working overtime on weekends without taking lunch breaks"], "Sustained emotional labor (surface acting) without authentic alignment (deep acting) causes emotional exhaustion and occupational burnout."),
    ("What is 'Deep Acting' versus 'Surface Acting' in emotional labor?", "Deep acting involves genuinely aligning one's internal feelings with required displays; Surface acting merely fakes outward emotional expressions", ["Deep acting is for dramatic films; Surface acting is for television", "Deep acting is done by executives; Surface acting by junior staff", "Deep acting requires makeup; Surface acting requires uniforms"], "Deep acting (empathizing genuinely with customer distress) creates authentic connection and protects workers from burnout far better than shallow faking."),
    ("What does 'Cognitive Dissonance' trigger when personal values conflict with organizational directives?", "Psychological tension and distress, driving the individual to change their behavior, rationalize their actions, or exit the organization", ["A sudden drop in physical human body temperature", "A complete loss of human spoken language memory", "An instant increase in corporate stock dividend payouts"], "Festinger showed that internal dissonance demands resolution: workers either compromise ethics, justify complicity, or whistleblow/resign."),
    ("What is 'Constructive Discontent' in workplace innovation competencies?", "A positive, productive dissatisfaction with the status quo that drives an individual to passionately discover better solutions", ["Complaining endlessly around office water coolers without action", "Filing false anonymous complaints against innocent coworkers", "Refusing to do any assigned work due to chronic personal anger"], "Constructive discontent refuses to accept mediocrity, channeling creative frustration into superior designs and processes."),
    ("What is 'Psychological Capital' (PsyCap) composed of (Luthans et al.)?", "HERO: Hope (goal-directed agency), Efficacy (confidence), Resilience (bounce-back), and Optimism (positive attributions)", ["Honesty, Ethics, Responsibility, and Openness", "Humility, Empathy, Respect, and Order", "Hierarchy, Enterprise, Revenue, and Ownership"], "PsyCap represents positive psychological resources that can be measured, developed, and leveraged for superior performance."),
    ("In PsyCap, what is 'Hope' defined as (C.R. Snyder)?", "A positive motivational state based on an interactively derived sense of successful Agency (goal-directed energy) and Pathways (planning to meet goals)", ["Wishing passively that a miracle will solve current project crises", "Buying multiple lottery tickets in hopes of winning money", "Waiting for the government to cancel all corporate taxes"], "Hope combines determination (willpower) with creative alternative routes (waypower) to circumvent unexpected barriers."),
    ("What is 'Learned Helplessness' (Martin Seligman) in employee behavior?", "A state of passive resignation where an individual ceases trying to improve a situation because past uncontrollable failures convinced them they have zero agency", ["A physical disease causing muscular weakness in human limbs", "The inability to operate modern smartphone touchscreens", "A training program designed to teach workers how to follow rules"], "Learned helplessness destroys initiative; breaking it requires cognitive reframing, small wins, and restoring perceived control."),
    ("What is 'Appreciative Inquiry' (David Cooperrider) in organizational development?", "A strengths-based approach focusing on discovering and amplifying what is working exceptionally well, rather than obsessing over deficits and faults", ["An audit investigating corrupt financial bribery transactions", "A police interrogation questioning criminal suspects", "An annual tax assessment conducted by government inspectors"], "Appreciative Inquiry utilizes the 4-D cycle (Discover, Dream, Design, Destiny) to ignite positive collective change.")
]

beh_hard = [
    ("In the McBer / McClelland Behavioral Competency methodology, what are 'Threshold Competencies' versus 'Differentiating Competencies'?", "Threshold: baseline skills required to perform the job adequately; Differentiating: behavioral traits that distinguish superior performers from average performers", ["Threshold: qualifications of junior staff; Differentiating: traits of directors", "Threshold: skills in software coding; Differentiating: skills in accounting", "Threshold: domestic language skills; Differentiating: foreign languages"], "Threshold competencies (e.g., basic technical knowledge) are prerequisites; differentiating behavioral competencies drive sustained superior performance."),
    ("In psychometrics and competency modeling, what is the 'Multi-Trait Multi-Method' (MTMM) matrix (Campbell and Fiske)?", "An analytical matrix evaluating Construct Validity by assessing convergent validity across different methods and discriminant validity across different traits", ["A statistical tool for calculating corporate quarterly dividend yields", "A spreadsheet tracking employee annual vacation days", "A database query optimizing search speeds in relational tables"], "MTMM proves that an assessment measures the targeted competency (e.g., leadership) and not merely the format of the test (e.g., self-report)."),
    ("In behavioral assessment scoring, what is 'Frame-of-Reference' (FOR) training for assessors?", "Standardizing raters' cognitive schemas by training them on specific behavioral benchmarks and definitions before they evaluate candidates", ["Training corporate actors on how to deliver theatrical stage lines", "Instructing interviewers on how to design decorative PowerPoint frames", "Teaching security guards how to monitor closed-circuit cameras"], "FOR training calibrates evaluator standards, significantly reducing idiosyncratic rating bias and elevating inter-rater reliability."),
    ("What is 'Inter-Rater Reliability' in assessment centers and competency evaluations?", "The degree of agreement, consistency, and consensus among independent evaluators assessing the same behavioral performance", ["The percentage of candidates who accept final corporate job offers", "The speed at which an interviewer completes the evaluation scorecard", "The correlation between candidate age and interview test scores"], "High inter-rater reliability (measured by Cohen's Kappa or intraclass correlation) ensures scoring is objective and not rater-dependent."),
    ("What is the 'Dunning-Kruger Effect' in cognitive and behavioral self-awareness?", "Cognitive bias where individuals with low competence in a domain substantially overestimate their own ability, while high performers underestimate theirs", ["A psychological phobia of speaking in front of public audiences", "The tendency to forget names of people met during business conferences", "A statistical relationship between height and leadership promotions"], "Incompetence deprives individuals of the metacognitive ability to recognize their own errors, breeding supreme false confidence."),
    ("In organizational justice theory, what are the four distinct dimensions of perceived workplace fairness (Colquitt)?", "Distributive Justice (outcomes), Procedural Justice (processes), Interpersonal Justice (respect), and Informational Justice (explanations)", ["Financial Justice, Legal Justice, Moral Justice, and Social Justice", "Executive Justice, Union Justice, Worker Justice, and Client Justice", "Past Justice, Present Justice, Future Justice, and Eternal Justice"], "High procedural and interactional justice foster deep institutional loyalty and organizational citizenship even when outcomes are unfavorable."),
    ("In organizational justice, what is 'Procedural Justice' primarily determined by (Thibaut and Walker)?", "Voice (having input in decisions), consistency, bias suppression, accuracy of information, correctability, and ethicality", ["The monetary size of the annual performance cash bonus check", "The physical luxury of the furniture in the corporate boardroom", "The number of days an employee is permitted to take sick leave"], "When employees perceive decision-making processes as transparent, fair, and consistent, they accept difficult outcomes with minimal resentment."),
    ("In organizational justice, what does 'Informational Justice' examine?", "Whether leaders provide candid, timely, comprehensive, and respectful explanations for decisions affecting employees", ["Whether the company purchases high-speed computer servers", "Whether employee payroll data is published on public internet sites", "Whether the corporate library has sufficient encyclopedias"], "Informational justice alleviates the pain of adverse events by honoring human dignity with transparent, honest rationales."),
    ("What is 'Organizational Cynicism' as a destructive behavioral response?", "A chronic negative attitude characterized by contempt, distrust, and belief that the organization lacks integrity and that leaders are self-serving", ["A constructive desire to test software code for security bugs", "An aggressive negotiating style used with external suppliers", "A humorous speech delivered by a stand-up comedian at lunch"], "Cynicism poisons culture, paralyzes change initiatives, and breeds widespread passive disengagement and turnover."),
    ("What is 'Moral Licensing' in psychological self-leadership and behavioral ethics?", "Subconsciously using past good deeds or ethical actions to justify subsequent selfish, questionable, or unethical behavior", ["Obtaining a statutory government permit to operate a casino", "Earning an accredited university degree in moral philosophy", "Receiving a written character recommendation from a religious minister"], "Moral licensing reveals human moral hypocrisy: celebrating past virtues makes people feel licensed to cut ethical corners later."),
    ("What is 'Moral Injury' in high-stakes professional service (e.g., healthcare, military, disaster management)?", "The profound psychological distress resulting from actions, or lack of actions, that violate one's deeply held moral values or ethical codes", ["A physical broken bone suffered during a workplace accident", "A financial penalty imposed for paying corporate taxes late", "A defamatory rumor published about an executive in newspapers"], "Moral injury shatters core identity and trust, occurring when individuals feel compelled to compromise ethics due to systemic failures."),
    ("In behavioral decision-making, what is 'Escalation of Commitment' (Barry Staw)?", "The tendency to allocate additional resources to a failing course of action because of prior investments, driven by self-justification and desire to avoid admitting failure", ["Promoting top-performing employees to executive director roles", "Increasing company product prices to cover rising raw material costs", "Adding extra security firewalls to protect computer database networks"], "Leaders escalate commitment to save face, rationalize earlier choices, and maintain internal consistency, driving catastrophic bankruptcies."),
    ("In behavioral ethics, what is 'Bounded Ethicality' (Chugh, Banaji, and Bazerman)?", "Psychological processes that lead people to engage in ethically questionable behaviors that contradict their own conscious ethical standards without awareness", ["Strict legal limits placed on corporate political campaign donations", "A formal code of conduct signed by company board directors", "The statutory limitation period for filing financial fraud lawsuits"], "Implicit biases, in-group favoritism, and cognitive blindness cause well-intentioned leaders to act unethically while believing they are moral."),
    ("In behavioral ethics, what is the 'Slippery Slope Effect'?", "The gradual erosion of ethical standards where minor, imperceptible compromises desensitize individuals, leading progressively to major catastrophic fraud", ["A physical wet floor hazard inside a factory warehouse", "A sudden unexpected drop in company stock market valuation", "A legal bankruptcy proceeding following contract cancellation"], "Major financial scandals (e.g., Enron) rarely start with bold mega-crimes; they begin with tiny, rationalized accounting fudges that escalate over time."),
    ("What is 'Social Loafing' (The Ringelmann Effect) in team dynamics?", "The tendency for individuals to exert less physical or cognitive effort when working collectively in a group than when working alone", ["Sitting on comfortable sofas in the office lounge during work hours", "Browsing social media websites while attending business webinars", "Taking extended lunch breaks with coworkers at local restaurants"], "Social loafing occurs when individual contributions are lost in the collective pool, preventable by establishing individual accountability."),
    ("How can a leader systematically eliminate 'Social Loafing' in team projects?", "By ensuring individual performance is visible, measurable, and uniquely accountable, and emphasizing the indispensability of each role", ["By having all team members submit work anonymously under a group name", "By threatening to fire the entire department if goals are not met", "By assigning the exact same repetitive task to five people simultaneously"], "When individual contributions are transparently tracked and valued, social loafing drops and individual ownership surges."),
    ("In behavioral economics, what is 'Loss Aversion' (Kahneman and Tversky, Prospect Theory)?", "The psychological phenomenon where the pain of losing something is experienced as roughly twice as intense as the joy of gaining the equivalent amount", ["The financial strategy of cutting loss-making factory product lines", "The fear of getting lost while driving through an unfamiliar city", "An accounting rule mandating write-down of depreciated machinery"], "Loss aversion explains why employees resist change: the perceived immediate loss of comfort looms larger than hypothetical future gains."),
    ("In behavioral decision-making, what is 'Endowment Effect' (Richard Thaler)?", "The cognitive bias where people assign a significantly higher value to objects, roles, or ideas simply because they own or hold them", ["A large financial donation made by an alumnus to a university", "An insurance policy that pays cash bonuses upon retirement", "The government grant awarded to research development laboratories"], "The endowment effect fuels territorial defensiveness: managers overvalue their existing projects and fight ferociously against restructuring."),
    ("In behavioral psychology, what is 'Reactance Theory' (Jack Brehm)?", "An unpleasant motivational arousal that emerges when people perceive that their behavioral freedoms are being threatened or restricted, driving rebellion", ["A technical chemical reaction occurring in industrial factories", "The electrical impedance measured across electronic circuits", "A medical allergic reaction to pharmaceutical antibiotics"], "Aggressive micromanagement and authoritarian decrees trigger psychological reactance, driving covert sabotage and stubborn defiance."),
    ("What is 'Emotional Contagion' in team leadership?", "The automatic, unconscious psychological process where individuals mimic and catch the emotional expressions, moods, and energy of those around them (especially leaders)", ["A biological virus transmitted through sneezing in open offices", "A computer virus spreading across company email network servers", "A financial crisis triggered by international currency fluctuations"], "A leader's anxiety or calm spreads rapidly through unconscious mimicry, setting the prevailing emotional temperature of the organization."),
    ("In self-leadership, what is 'Cognitive Flexibility'?", "The mental ability to switch between thinking about two different concepts, and to consider multiple concepts simultaneously to adapt to novel situations", ["The physical ability to bend one's body during gymnastic exercises", "The speed at which a computer processor executes calculation cycles", "The willingness to change working hours from morning to evening"], "Cognitive flexibility dismantles functional fixedness, allowing thinkers to recombine existing knowledge to tackle unprecedented disruptions."),
    ("What is 'Functional Fixedness' in creative problem solving (Karl Duncker)?", "A cognitive bias that limits an individual to perceiving and using an object or concept only in the traditional way it is customarily used", ["A permanent malfunction in a mechanical factory machine", "A rigid corporate salary pay grade that never increases", "A strict legal clause in a government procurement contract"], "Overcoming functional fixedness requires abstracting the fundamental physical or logical properties of tools to invent novel uses."),
    ("What is 'Fundamental Attribution Error' (Actor-Observer Bias) in interpersonal perception?", "Overemphasizing internal personality flaws when explaining others' mistakes, while attributing one's own mistakes to external situational factors", ["Making an arithmetic calculation error on a company balance sheet", "Assuming all foreign citizens speak the identical native language", "Believing that tomorrow's weather will be identical to yesterday's"], "When a colleague is late, we judge them lazy; when we are late, we blame traffic. Counteracting it requires cognitive empathy."),
    ("What is 'Self-Serving Bias' in organizational performance evaluation?", "Attributing positive successes to one's own internal talent and effort, while attributing negative failures to external bad luck or others", ["Eating lunch before attending scheduled executive board meetings", "Taking care of one's personal physical health and wellness", "Filing corporate expense reimbursement vouchers on time"], "Self-serving bias blinds leaders to their own blind spots, preventing meaningful developmental growth from failures."),
    ("What is 'Group Polarization' in team decision-making?", "The tendency for group discussion to intensify and shift the group's collective decision toward a more extreme position than individuals initially held", ["Splitting a corporate team into two separate regional branches", "Conducting democratic voting where the majority always wins", "Moving office furniture to opposite ends of the conference room"], "Deliberation among like-minded individuals amplifies initial inclinations, pushing groups toward radical riskiness or hyper-conservatism."),
    ("What is 'In-Group Favoritism' (Social Identity Theory - Henri Tajfel)?", "The tendency to favor, trust, and allocate more resources to members of one's own perceived group compared to out-group members", ["Promoting employees based strictly on objective merit exams", "Purchasing raw materials from local domestic manufacturers", "Treating all human beings with identical professional dignity"], "Minimal group paradigms show that even arbitrary, trivial divisions trigger immediate in-group favoritism and out-group derogation."),
    ("In executive behavior, what is 'The Peter Principle' (Laurence J. Peter)?", "'In a hierarchy, every employee tends to rise to their level of incompetence'", ["'Work expands to fill the time available for its completion'", "'If anything can go wrong, it will go wrong at the worst time'", "'Adding manpower to a late software project makes it later'"], "Promoting individuals based on success in their current role rather than competencies required for the next role results in widespread leadership dysfunction."),
    ("In behavioral design, what is 'Nudge Theory' (Thaler and Sunstein)?", "Indirectly influencing the choice architecture and behavior of individuals in predictable ways without forbidding any options or changing economic incentives", ["Forcing people to obey laws through strict criminal prison penalties", "Shoving someone physically to make them move faster in an office", "Paying cash financial bribes to voters during public elections"], "Nudges (e.g., setting optimal defaults like organ donation opt-out or retirement automatic enrollment) steer choices while preserving freedom."),
    ("What is 'Choice Architecture' in behavioral interventions?", "The careful design of the environment, presentation, and context in which decisions are presented to human decision-makers", ["The physical architectural blueprint of an executive office suite", "The mathematical calculation of computer memory database tables", "The structural engineering of public suspension bridges"], "Altering choice architecture (e.g., placing healthy fruit at eye level in cafeterias) nudges subconscious decisions without mandates."),
    ("In organizational behavioral health, what is 'Compassion Fatigue' (Secondary Traumatic Stress)?", "Emotional, physical, and spiritual exhaustion resulting from chronic exposure to and empathy for others' trauma and suffering", ["Getting tired of walking around large corporate office campuses", "Losing interest in reading theoretical academic literature", "Feeling bored during routine administrative weekly meetings"], "Common in healthcare, social welfare, and crisis leadership, compassion fatigue impairs judgment unless mitigated by intentional boundaries."),
    ("What is 'Imposter Phenomenon' root cause in high-achieving behavioral dynamics?", "Internalized perfectionism, pluralistic ignorance of others' private struggles, and attributing triumphs to external luck rather than internal competence", ["Having fraudulent fake degrees purchased from diploma mills", "A deliberate criminal attempt to impersonate an executive", "A genuine lack of basic technical knowledge in the domain"], "High achievers often hide vulnerability; open culture and transparent mentoring dismantle the myth that everyone else has effortless mastery."),
    ("What is 'Grit' as formulated by Angela Duckworth?", "A sustained passion and perseverance for long-term goals, maintaining effort and interest over years despite failure and plateaus", ["Raw cognitive IQ and natural born athletic talent", "Working intensely for 48 hours without taking any breaks", "Shouting aggressively to intimidate opponents during debates"], "Duckworth showed that grit predicts achievement in demanding arenas far more reliably than raw baseline talent or IQ."),
    ("What is 'Locus of Meaning' in Victor Frankl's logotherapy applied to workplace resilience?", "The discovery of purpose, personal responsibility, and meaning even in the face of inevitable suffering and adversity", ["A statistical indicator measuring corporate financial profitability", "The physical geographic location of corporate headquarters", "The legal jurisdiction where corporate taxes are audited"], "Frankl proved that humans can endure almost any 'how' if they hold a deep personal 'why'—meaning is chosen, not given.")
]
